[Music]  marvel studios shanxi and the legend of  the 10 rings introduces some of the  newest most dynamic artifacts in the  marvel cinematic universe the ten rings  so i'm getting the inside scoop from vfx  supervisor christopher townsend hey  chris hey how are you doing all right  chris let's get into it now what was the  process of reimagining the look and the  powers of the ten rings uh one of the  interesting aspects of obviously this  film with other ten rings and their  powers in the comics they're represented  as finger rings and we wanted to go more  onto almost bracelets and rings around  the wrist we started looking into the  idea of having each individual ring be  representative of a particular power  as it's represented in the comics but  again decided we didn't really want to  do that and we wanted to keep it a  little bit more simple and show them as  just a more unified sort of presence of  what these rings are doing and we  started going down this journey of not  really being about the rings themselves  that are powerful but rather it's the  energy that they emanate that was going  to be the power  right so once you have that look and  movement how do you then communicate  that with the performers on set do you  give them something that they can  actually physically wear  so there's a lot of development trying  to figure out exactly what we should  build as as the onset pieces for the  rings it was a real challenge trying to  figure out what should they look like  and should they be going small to large  and eventually we decided that they  would need to be changing and animating  so for the vast majority of the shots  that you'll see the rings even when  they're just rattling around on his  wrists they're actually fully computer  generated  the rings are so dynamic especially  because they emit a different color  dependent on who's wielding them what  was the process of choosing which  character would wield which color so  trying to find the correct colors for  shanxi when he uses the rings and for  when he uses the rings was kind of  tricky coming down to color specifically  we ended up with the golds for shangji  and sort of a purplish blue for when woo  as our sort of two key colors  so when we see that big final climactic  battle between changshi and wen wu we  really get to see how the ten rings emit  different powers when they're wielded in  different fighting styles could you  point out some of those subtle  differences so i think one of the  interesting things with the film is how  the rings are different for each  character in essence when woo is not  necessarily fundamentally evil but there  are sort of aggressive overtones to who  he is as a character and so the  characterization of those rings was an  extension of that and when shanxi  when he's using the rings there's almost  an aura of goodness about him so when  he's in a almost a tug of war moment  with his father we see when woohoo uses  the sort of lightning-based energy very  spiky and aggressive relative to  champions almost silk ribbons of glowing  energy and flowing and it's a much more  gentle serene power  this scene really has so many layers  because there's this intense action  paired with these really powerful  emotional moments what role does vfx  play in helping to create and support  those kinds of layers so we use a lot of  what we call previous pre-visualization  where we will try and figure or plan out  a particular scene so that we know  exactly where it's going to end up we  also in this case used a lot of stunt  biz so the stunt team would go away and  they would enact the entire sequence and  once you've figured out what the move is  you're then also at the same time  figuring out what the camera is doing  and how the camera reacts to the stunts  and how it reacts to the stunts with the  visual effects that are ultimately  placed on it once you've actually  finished photographing in principle  photography and you've got these  particular you know stunt fights you  know the sequences it's then our task to  start assembling it and putting it into  the scene  the world itself doesn't exist it's a  fully computer generated world for a lot  of this stuff particularly when you're  out on the what we call the mountain of  souls in front of the gate so we've  already planned it and we've prepped it  so that everyone knows exactly you know  what it's going to feel like but we  ultimately ended up replacing all of it  we have to track the camera and figure  out where the camera is in relation to  the actors and in relation to the world  that does exist in their the ground  playing all that kind of stuff and what  we're doing with the visual effects is  trying to enhance that and help further  that story arc and that characterization  it really is such an awesome ride what  do you hope that fans feel when they  walk away from that film i hope that  when the fans have watched the film that  they come away  thinking that not only they had a really  good ride that they've seen some  incredible fights but that ultimately  it's about relationships and it's about  love and family it's just got so much  going on and i think it's really  exciting to be working on a film that  has so many different  touch points that hopefully an audience  can get excited about  oh thank you so much chris and of course  everyone go see marvel studios chongqi  and the legend of the 10 rings in  theaters now  [Music]  [Music]  you

